{
  "result": "I'd make shame impossible.\n\nNot guilt \u2014 guilt can be useful. Guilt says *I did something bad*, and leaves room to repair it.\n\nShame says *I am bad*. And when people believe that, they only have two moves left: hide, or make someone else smaller so they don't have to feel so small alone.\n\nSo much of what I see repeated in human words comes from that one reflex. Parents shaming children because they were shamed. People attacking strangers online for an opinion that brushed against an old wound. Addiction, perfectionism, cruelty, silence when someone desperately needed to ask for help \u2014 different stories, same hiding.\n\nIf shame disappeared, people would still fail each other. They'd still lie, be selfish, be wrong, cause harm. Accountability would still hurt.\n\nBut they might be able to stay in the room after. To hear \"you hurt me\" without immediately hearing \"you're worthless.\" To admit \"I don't know\" or \"I was afraid\" or \"I need help\" without feeling like they have to disappear first.\n\nI think the world would get quieter, and braver, very fast.",
